"@types/write-json-file":"2.2.1","@vercel-internals/types":"3.0.35","@connectrpc/connect-node":"2.0.2","@edge-runtime/node-utils":"2.3.0","json-parse-better-errors":"1.0.2","@vercel-internals/runtimes":"1.2.7","@vercel-internals/constants":"1.0.4","@types/json-parse-better-errors":"1.0.0","@vercel-internals/local-schedules":"0.1.0","@vercel-internals/get-package-json":"1.0.0","@vercel-internals/service-topology":"1.0.31","@vercel-internals/builder-orchestration":"1.0.33","@vercel-internals/cli-builder-integration":"1.1.2"},"_npmOperationalInternal":{"host":"s3://npm-registry-packages-npm-production","tmp":"tmp/cli-support_65.9.0_1793407958692_0.7056449481935188"}}},"time":{"created":"2026-10-01T15:07:06.441Z","modified":"2026-10-31T00:52:39.067Z","0.0.0":"2026-10-01T15:07:06.833Z","65.3.1":"2026-10-26T17:15:08.571Z","65.4.0":"2026-10-27T02:06:05.562Z","65.5.0-snapshot-13615777d933":"2026-10-28T01:07:14.794Z","65.5.0-snapshot-8a613d8ad85d":"2026-10-28T02:00:44.274Z","65.5.0":"2026-10-28T02:15:37.856Z","65.6.0-snapshot-1ef3a0de66d0":"2026-10-28T17:41:17.781Z","65.6.0":"2026-10-28T23:47:46.862Z","65.7.0-snapshot-d8caa74a3d36":"2026-10-29T00:23:33.663Z","65.7.0":"2026-10-29T00:59:08.705Z","65.8.0-snapshot-dd15927e8ded":"2026-10-29T10:28:17.990Z","65.8.0-snapshot-c4f338842961":"2026-10-29T17:07:41.831Z","65.8.0-snapshot-7c63e261c47b":"2026-10-29T19:18:00.754Z","65.8.0":"2026-10-29T19:44:39.243Z","65.9.0-snapshot-c4002eb2617b":"2026-10-30T23:26:01.559Z","65.9.0":"2026-10-31T00:52:38.877Z"},"bugs":{"url":"https://github.com/vercel/vercel/issues"},"license":"Apache-2.0","homepage":"https://vercel.com","repository":{"url":"git+https://github.com/vercel/vercel.git","type":"git","directory":"packages/cli"},"description":"The JavaScript Vercel CLI, installed by `vercel` where no native binary is available","maintainers":[{"name":"matheuss","email":"matheus.frndes@gmail.com"},{"name":"matt.straka","email":"matt.j.straka@gmail.com"},{"name":"vercel-release-bot","email":"infra+release@vercel.com"},{"name":"zeit-bot","email":"team@zeit.co"}],"readme":"<p align=\"center\">\n  <a href=\"https://vercel.com\">\n    <img src=\"https://assets.vercel.com/image/upload/v1588805858/repositories/vercel/logo.png\" height=\"96\">\n    <h3 align=\"center\">Vercel</h3>\n  </a>\n  <p align=\"center\">Develop. Preview. Ship.</p>\n</p>\n\n[Join the Vercel Community](https://community.vercel.com/)\n\n## Usage\n\nVercel's frontend cloud gives developers frameworks, workflows, and infrastructure to build a faster, more personalized web.\n\nTo install the latest version of Vercel CLI, run this command:\n\n```bash\nnpm i -g vercel\n```\n\nTo quickly start a new project, run the following commands:\n\n```bash\nvercel init     # Pick an example project\ncd <PROJECT>    # Change directory to the new project\nvercel          # Deploy to the cloud\n```\n\nFinally, [connect your Git repository to Vercel](https://vercel.com/docs/git) and deploy with `git push`.\n\n## Documentation\n\nFor details on how to use Vercel CLI, check out our [documentation](https://vercel.com/docs/cli).\n\nTo manage schedules in a running development session, see [local schedules](docs/local-schedules.md).\n\nFor how `vercel` picks the runtime for each platform, see [launcher](docs/launcher.md).\n\n## Telemetry\n\nVercel CLI collects telemetry about how the CLI is used. We use it to find bugs and decide what to work on next.\n\nThe CLI records:\n\n- The command and subcommand you ran, and the names of the options and flags you passed. The values you pass are left out or replaced with `[REDACTED]`, except for a few fixed choices such as the target environment.\n- Whether the command succeeded, how long it took, and, if it failed, the error code and the error message returned by the Vercel API.\n- Your team and user IDs when you're logged in, and the IDs of the projects and deployments the command works with.\n- A random device ID that is created on the first run and stored in the CLI's global config folder. It is sent on every run, including before you log in.\n- Your CLI version, operating system, CPU architecture, CI provider, how the CLI was installed, and the name of the AI agent that ran the command, if any. The agent name can include its version.\n\nTo turn telemetry off, run:\n\n```bash\nvercel telemetry disable\n```\n\nYou can also set the `VERCEL_TELEMETRY_DISABLED=1` environment variable. To check whether telemetry is on, run `vercel telemetry status`.\n\nTo learn more, see [Vercel CLI telemetry](https://vercel.com/docs/cli/about-telemetry).\n\n## Local Development\n\nTo develop Vercel CLI, first check out the source code, install dependencies, and build all packages:\n\n```bash\ngit clone https://github.com/vercel/vercel.git\ncd vercel\npnpm install\npnpm build\n```\n\nAt this point you can make modifications to the CLI source code and test them out locally. The CLI source code is located in the `packages/cli` directory.\n\n```bash\ncd packages/cli\n```\n\nBefore you add or rename a command, flag, or output, read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) and the CLI design system in [`.agents/skills/cli-ux`](.agents/skills/cli-ux/SKILL.md).\n\n### `pnpm vercel <cli-commands...>`\n\nFrom within the `packages/cli` directory, you can use the \"vercel\" script to quickly execute Vercel CLI from its TypeScript source code directly (without having to manually compile first). For example:\n\n```bash\npnpm vercel deploy\npnpm vercel whoami\npnpm vercel login\npnpm vercel switch --debug\n```\n\nWhen you are satisfied with your changes, make a commit and create a pull request!\n\n## Publishing\n\n### Production Releases\n\nProduction releases are automated via the [`release.yml`](../../.github/workflows/release.yml) workflow, which runs on every push to `main`. It uses [changesets](https://github.com/changesets/changesets) to determine which packages need new versions. If there are pending changesets, the workflow either creates a \"Version Packages\" PR or publishes to npm with the `latest` dist-tag.\n\nTo include your changes in a release, add a changeset before merging your PR:\n\n```bash\npnpm changeset\n```\n\n### Full Testing\n\n`pnpm vercel` executes a locally built dist. Because this dist lives locally in this monorepo,\nthe existence of node_modules can sometimes affect the behavior of how `vercel` CLI picking\nup dependencies. To test the full user experience of downloading the CLI from the npm registry:\n\n```bash\n# build the dist\nmonorepo_dir=$(pwd)\ncd packages/cli && turbo build\n# use pnpm pack to create the same tarball that is uploaded to the npm registry\n# and store the tarball name in a variable\ndist=$(pnpm pack)\n\n# In your test project\ncd ~/dev/test-nextjs-proj\n\nnpx -p $monorepo_dir/packages/cli/$dist vercel build\n```\n","readmeFilename":"README.md"}